      "writtenOn": "2022-08-16T20:29:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Shouldn\u0027t the virtual mux driver have sent the USB mux event when the mux changed?  Why should a hard reset clear affect the USB mux events, when it\u0027s not maintained in the PD_STATUS_* events?",
      "revId": "2d996a8fe651530a224f301215ff2f8b147bf65c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "74fa7fa8_4b3c8e85",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pd_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 133,
      "author": {
        "id": 1182824
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-17T06:54:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The comment might be not clear enough.\n\nThe problem is that when AP boots, EC would send a batch of Type-C events. The events occurred at EC side has a sequence, but AP doesn\u0027t have the knowledge of the sequence. [CL: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromiumos/third_party/kernel/+/3475554/2]e.g.\n\n1. EC occured hard reset\n2. EC configured mux\n3. AP boots. AP received mux changed event and hard reset event.\n4. cros-ec-typec handle the mux event first\n5. cros-ec-typec handle the hard reset event, and hence reset USB mux config.\n\nAt EC side, the mux status doesn\u0027t change after the hard reset.",
      "parentUuid": "45c36298_aed5ab07",
      "revId": "2d996a8fe651530a224f301215ff2f8b147bf65c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7ff2a444_b9b3410a",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pd_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 133,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-17T15:46:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Adding Prashant for his opinion, but it seems like this should be fixed from a kernel level?",
      "parentUuid": "74fa7fa8_4b3c8e85",
      "revId": "2d996a8fe651530a224f301215ff2f8b147bf65c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5a350330_fe40c1ff",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pd_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 133,
      "author": {
        "id": 1181774
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-17T17:59:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Adding Prashant for his opinion, but it seems like this should be fixed from a kernel level?\n\nI recall we had a related problem with one of these boards (may it was a different board, I\u0027m not sure), and we moved the mux configuration step to the beginning of the port update routine [1]\n\n\u003e 1. EC occured hard reset\n\u003e 2. EC configured mux\n\u003e 3. AP boots. AP received mux changed event and hard reset event.\n\nDo you have a chip reset hook for these boards? The EC can/should clear reset events when a reboot/boot occurs, since those are irrelevant because:\n- The EC has moved on and started the PD negotiation process again (in particular 2.) means that the reset from 1.) should be considered \"stale\")\n- Resets that occured during a prior boot cycle are not of much use to the AP.\n\nOrdinarily, i.e during normal use, the HARD_RESET would come in \"in sequence\" and be followed by the SOP{_PRIME}_DISC_DONE events so the AP has enough time to re-register everything.\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/chrome-platform/20220208184721.1697194-4-pmalani@chromium.org/\n\nAlso, may I ask what is causing this hard reset in the first place?",
      "parentUuid": "7ff2a444_b9b3410a",
      "revId": "2d996a8fe651530a224f301215ff2f8b147bf65c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2cc48541_1d4cedf1",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pd_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 133,
      "author": {
        "id": 1182824
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-24T02:04:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Also, may I ask what is causing this hard reset in the first place?\n\nWe had a discussion at b/224471170.\n\nTo make it short, we follow USB SPEC `10.3.1.1 DSPORT.Powered-off` which recommends to cycle VBUS when the USB HUB initialized when there are sink devices connected. We do this by sending a `HARDRESET` event. This USB XHCI hub is initialized at AP firmware, so the `HARDRESET` will be sent out at the stage. AP handles the event at starting up the kernel, so the kernel will see a batch of events including `HARDRESET`. However, AP has no knowledge of the event sequence, so it handles the `HARDRESET` event in the last place which clears the previously set USB MUX status.\n\n\nFor questions from Prashant:\n\n\u003e Do you have a chip reset hook for these boards? The EC can/should clear reset events when a reboot/boot occurs, since those are irrelevant because:\n\u003e The EC has moved on and started the PD negotiation process again (in particular 2.) means that the reset from 1.) should be considered \"stale\")\n\nFrom the point of view of the EC, the `HARDRESET` and `MUX` events are not happened at the different boots, there are no power status transitions when these events occurred.  So EC should propagate these events to the AP honestly, and also, the `HARDRESET` events might be occurred due to many reasons (e.g. the external device triggered hard reset), since EC has no knowledge of cros-ec-typec status, I think EC should not filter out the status.",
      "parentUuid": "5a350330_fe40c1ff",
      "revId": "2d996a8fe651530a224f301215ff2f8b147bf65c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ede52977_559fcf50",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pd_host.c",
